{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a30d1788641e71a5a","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_019Z7E5mXXfunZPbc9KUk9mz","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-0eE2Fw-translated-rust/a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2/tasks/a30d1788641e71a5a.output","summary":"Written to `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/tmp/vwork/errors_g3.md`.\n\n## Verification\n- `wc -l` = **254**\n- `grep -c '^|'` = **254**\n- Row count = **254** (numbering verified sequential 1..254 with an awk check; zero mismatches)\n- Per-file: jsarray.c 63, jsobject.c 50, jsstring.c 90, jsnumber.c 32, jsboolean.c 4, jsbuiltin.c 15\n- Nothing under `c_src/` was modified (read-only greps and Reads only)\n\n## Key findings\n\n**No `assert()` calls exist in any of the six files** — I grepped explicitly. Asserts in this subsystem live in `jsrun.c` (e.g. `jsR_setarrayindex` at jsrun.c:673-674, 678).\n\n**Array length RangeErrors are not raised in jsarray.c.** `RangeError \"invalid array length\"` and `RangeError \"array too large\"` are thrown at `jsrun.c:707` / `jsrun.c:709` (and `jsrun.c:676`, message `\"array too large\"`, from `jsR_setarrayindex` against `JS_ARRAYLIMIT` = 1<<26). jsarray.c only *reaches* them, via `js_setlength` (jsarray.c:19) and `jsB_new_Array`'s single-numeric-arg path (jsarray.c:31) — rows 2-5 record this with the cross-file attribution.\n\n**`js_toobject(J, 0)` is a large implicit rejection surface.** Every `Np_*`, `Bp_*`, `Sp_toString/valueOf`, and `Op_hasOwnProperty/isPrototypeOf/propertyIsEnumerable` entry point calls it on `this` before any explicit check, yielding TypeError `\"cannot convert undefined to object\"` / `\"cannot convert null to object\"` (jsvalue.c:401-402). I gave each call site its own row since a Rust port must reproduce these exact messages, distinct from the `checkstring` message.\n\n**`checkstring` (jsstring.c:15) has 17 call sites**, all producing TypeError `\"string function called on null or undefined\"`. Enumerated one row per String.prototype method as requested.\n\n**Three genuine defects surfaced while enumerating** (recorded as rows 138, 179, 69):\n- `jsstring.c:253` — `Sp_substring_imp` installs `js_try` whose handler does `js_free(J, p)`, but `p` is declared uninitialised at line 228 and is not assigned until line 258. An OOM inside `js_malloc(J, UTFmax + (tail-head))` frees an indeterminate pointer.\n- `jsstring.c:605` — `if (x > 10)` should be `x >= 10`; an out-of-range `$10` back-reference emits `'0' + 10` == `':'`, i.e. `$:` instead of `$10`.\n- `jsobject.c:27` — `Op_toString`'s `switch (self->type)` has no `default`; an unenumerated type pushes nothing and the C-function result is read off the stack top (`this`).\n\n**Two spec deviations in the URI state machines** (rows 247, 252): `Encode` and `Decode` in jsbuiltin.c operate on raw bytes and never validate UTF-8 well-formedness, so the only URIErrors possible are `\"truncated escape sequence\"` (jsbuiltin.c:145) and `\"invalid escape sequence\"` (jsbuiltin.c:149). ES5's lone-surrogate/malformed-sequence URIError paths are absent.\n\n**Also worth flagging for the port:** `S_fromCharCode` (jsstring.c:455) does `js_malloc(J, (top-1) * UTFmax + 1)` with no `JS_STRLIMIT` check and a product that can overflow `int`; and `Sp_split`'s default limit is the magic `1 << 30` (jsstring.c:727, 782) rather than 2^32-1.","usage":{"total_tokens":91978,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":365525},"uuid":"39be690f-6b6a-402f-be08-bb0f5f462b2c","session_id":"a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2"}
