{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_3cliednllvnnnt2dhcct76emrcm6lj7vpkagnkmbgawahbgofysq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01SSnq5WCrbjCp3GLxjJr9Qz","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Write js_builtins differential tests","subagent_type":"general-purpose","run_in_background":false,"prompt":"Work in /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust\n\nCONTEXT\nThis is a C->Rust translation of MuJS being verified by DIFFERENTIAL TESTING.\n`c_src/` holds the C ground truth (READ-ONLY, NEVER MODIFY, and never write any\nnew file anywhere under c_src/). `src/*.rs` is the Rust translation, built as a\ncdylib. A test harness already exists at `tests/common/mod.rs` which dlopens\nBOTH `c_src/build/libmujs.so` and `target/debug/libmujs.so` and exposes every\nexported symbol as a method. Existing passing examples you MUST read first for\nstyle: `tests/ll_num.rs`, `tests/ll_regexp.rs`, `tests/ll_lex.rs`, `tests/smoke.rs`.\n\nHARNESS API (in tests/common/mod.rs -- read it, ~1250 lines)\n  libs() -> &'static Pair          // Pair { c: Lib, rs: Lib }\n  new_state(l, flags) -> JS        // installs a report hook + `print` and `dump`\n                                   // globals. `print(...)` joins js_tostring of\n                                   // its args; `dump(...)` uses js_torepr.\n  eval(l, flags, src) -> EvalResult { load_rc, call_rc, result, out, top }\n  dostring(l, flags, src) -> (c_int, String)\n  diff_eval(flags, src) / diff_dostring(flags, src)   // assert C == Rust\n  set_cur(l)                       // call before entering a library\n  cstr / from_c / out_take\n  Rng::new(seed) with .next_u32/.below/.range/.f64_sane/.f64_any/\n      .ascii_string/.unicode_string/.raw_bytes\n  with_big_stack(fn_item)          // 512MB stack; needed for deep recursion\n  Lib also exposes every low-level symbol, e.g. l.js_newregexp(j, pat, flags),\n  l.js_regcomp(...), l.pred(\"js_isregexp\", j, idx), l.nullary(...).\n\nYOUR TASK\nCreate ONE new file `tests/js_builtins.rs` with differential tests covering the\nrows of `CONFIGS.md` in the ranges 88-110 and 207-253, driven through the JS\nlayer (`diff_dostring` / `diff_eval`) AND through the low-level C entry points\nwhere they exist:\n  - js_newregexp with all 8 combinations of JS_REGEXP_G|JS_REGEXP_I|JS_REGEXP_M,\n    then RegExp.prototype.exec/test/toString and .source/.global/.ignoreCase/\n    .multiline/.lastIndex. Assert the lastIndex progression across repeated\n    exec() calls, with and without the global flag, and after manually assigning\n    lastIndex (including to a negative, huge, non-integer and non-numeric value).\n    Also call the exported `js_RegExp_prototype_exec` path via JS.\n  - String.prototype.split with a string separator, a regexp separator, no\n    separator, an empty separator, and with limit absent / 0 / small / huge /\n    negative / non-numeric.\n  - String.prototype.replace with a string pattern and a regexp pattern\n    (global and non-global), and a string replacement containing $$, $&, $`,\n    $', $1..$9, $10..$99 and a trailing bare $, AND a function replacement.\n    NOTE: jsstring.c:605 has `if (x > 10)` where the ES spec wants `x >= 10`;\n    reproduce whatever the C does, do not \"fix\" it.\n  - String.prototype.match / search / indexOf / lastIndexOf / slice / substring /\n    substr / charAt / charCodeAt / concat / toUpperCase / toLowerCase / trim /\n    localeCompare, over randomized ASCII AND non-ASCII (multi-byte UTF-8)\n    strings and over out-of-range and negative indices.\n  - String.fromCharCode with 0, 1, many args and with out-of-range code points.\n  - Array.prototype: sort with and without a comparator (and with a comparator\n    that returns nonsense / throws), join, concat, slice, splice, push, pop,\n    shift, unshift, reverse, indexOf, lastIndexOf, every, some, forEach, map,\n    filter, reduce, reduceRight, toString. Use dense arrays, SPARSE arrays\n    (created via `delete a[i]` and via `a.length = n`), arrays with a\n    non-index property, and empty/one/many element arrays -- this exercises the\n    flat-vs-unflattened array representation in jsrun.c/jsvalue.c.\n  - JSON.stringify with replacer absent / function / array, and space absent /\n    number 0..12 / string / long string, over nested objects and arrays and\n    every value type (including cyclic structures, undefined, functions, NaN,\n    Infinity, -0, Date objects, wrapper objects, and strings needing escaping).\n  - JSON.parse with and without a reviver, over every JSON value shape.\n  - Date: `new Date()` is NOT deterministic -- avoid it, or only compare\n    structure. DO test `new Date(ms)`, `new Date(str)` and\n    `new Date(y,m,d,h,mi,s,ms)` with 1..7 args including out-of-range and\n    fractional components; getTime/getFullYear/getMonth/getDate/getDay/getHours/\n    getMinutes/getSeconds/getMilliseconds/getTimezoneOffset and all the UTC\n    variants; setTime/setFullYear/.../setUTCHours; toString/toISOString/\n    toUTCString/toDateString/toTimeString/toJSON/valueOf; Date.parse and\n    Date.UTC. Include the invalid-date path (NaN time) for every getter and\n    formatter. NOTE: jsdate.c:748 Dp_setUTCHours defaults the minutes argument\n    from HourFromTime(t) rather than MinFromTime(t) -- that is the C's\n    behaviour and the Rust MUST match it.\n    IMPORTANT: local-timezone functions depend on the TZ environment variable;\n    both libraries run in the same process so they see the same TZ. That is\n    fine. Do not set TZ per-library.\n  - Math: every method over randomized doubles including inf/nan/-0 and the\n    exact boundary values, plus Math.random() must NOT be compared for value\n    (only that it stays in [0,1)).\n  - Number.prototype.toFixed / toExponential / toPrecision / toString(radix)\n    over randomized doubles and every digit count 0..21 and radix 2..36.\n\nRULES\n  - Every call must go through the