** — `Sp_replace_regexp`'s *function*-replacement arm does `for (x = 0; m.sub[x].sp; ++x)`, relying on a NULL terminator that only exists because `regexec` clears all `REG_MAXSUB`==16 slots. A regexp with the maximum 15 capture groups where **all** participate fills `sub[0..15]`, so it reads `m.sub[16]` — past the end of the stack-local `Resub` — and pushes a garbage pointer. **Both `libmujs.so` builds SIGSEGV on it** (I hit it, isolated it to each library separately, then capped that variant at 13 groups). All other capture walks (`Sp_split_regexp`, `js_RegExp_prototype_exec`, the string-replacement arm) are bounded by `m.nsub` and are tested up to 15 groups.\n- **`c_src/src/jsarray.c:279`, `:365`** — `Ap_sort_cmp`/`Ap_sort_swap` read `u.a.simple`/`u.a.array` without checking the object class, so `Array.prototype.sort.call(nonArray, …)` reads a never-stored union member.\n- **`c_src/src/jsstring.c:253-258`** — `Sp_substring_imp`'s `js_try` handler frees `p` before it is assigned (OOM-only).\n- **`c_src/src/jsdate.c:319-347`** — `fmtdate`/`fmttime` convert a NaN time to `int` and overflow `365*(y-1970)` *before* the `isfinite` guard; results are discarded and `d2i` in `src/jsi.rs` already reproduces gcc's `cvttsd2si`, so the invalid-date path is tested.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n1. **The batch helper is hardened against vacuous passes.** My first version had 4 tests whose generated scripts contained syntax errors (e.g. `.split(, 0)`, `.slice(1, )`, `JSON.parse(''a'')`); both libraries reported the identical syntax error, so the batches \"passed\" without executing anything. `diff_lines_n` now asserts `load_rc == 0`, `call_rc == 0` and a floor on emitted output lines, which surfaced all four; they are fixed.\n2. **TZ is pinned once, process-wide** (`XYZ5:30`, offset +330 min) inside a `OnceLock` before either library caches `LocalTZA`, with an assertion that both libraries agree. Without it a UTC host leaves `LocalTime()`/`UTC()`/`getTimezoneOffset` and `fmttime`'s signed-offset arm completely untested. `fmttime`'s `tza > 0` arm is unreachable from a single process (the only non-zero `tza` any caller passes is the once-cached `LocalTZA()`), which is noted in the file. The warm-up also removes a pre-existing latent race: `LocalTZA` uses `gmtime`/`localtime`, which share one static `struct tm` in libc, so two parallel tests seeding the two libraries' caches could have produced different offsets."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9f3c7aa379af7bd5 (use SendMessage with to: 'a9f3c7aa379af7bd5', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 329759\ntool_uses: 106\nduration_ms: 2226053</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2","uuid":"99c014e8-acfe-4421-9ba0-93752e49121f","timestamp":"2026-08-23T09:47:45.274Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Work in /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust\n\nCONTEXT\nThis is a C->Rust translation of MuJS being verified by DIFFERENTIAL TESTING.\n`c_src/` holds the C ground truth (READ-ONLY, NEVER MODIFY, and never write any\nnew file anywhere under c_src/). `src/*.rs` is the Rust translation, built as a\ncdylib. A test harness already exists at `tests/common/mod.rs` which dlopens\nBOTH `c_src/build/libmujs.so` and `target/debug/libmujs.so` and exposes every\nexported symbol as a method. Existing passing examples you MUST read first for\nstyle: `tests/ll_num.rs`, `tests/ll_regexp.rs`, `tests/ll_lex.rs`, `tests/smoke.rs`.\n\nHARNESS API (in tests/common/mod.rs -- read it, ~1250 lines)\n  libs() -> &'static Pair          // Pair { c: Lib, rs: Lib }\n  new_state(l, flags) -> JS        // installs a report hook + `print` and `dump`\n                                   // globals. `print(...)` joins js_tostring of\n                                   // its args; `dump(...)` uses js_torepr.\n  eval(l, flags, src) -> EvalResult { load_rc, call_rc, result, out, top }\n  dostring(l, flags, src) -> (c_int, String)\n  diff_eval(flags, src) / diff_dostring(flags, src)   // assert C == Rust\n  set_cur(l)                       // call before entering a library\n  cstr / from_c / out_take\n  Rng::new(seed) with .next_u32/.below/.range/.f64_sane/.f64_any/\n      .ascii_string/.unicode_string/.raw_bytes\n  with_big_stack(fn_item)          // 512MB stack; needed for deep recursion\n  Lib also exposes every low-level symbol, e.g. l.js_newregexp(j, pat, flags),\n  l.js_regcomp(...), l.pred(\"js_isregexp\", j, idx), l.nullary(...).\n\nYOUR TASK\nCreate ONE new file `tests/js_builtins.rs` with differential tests covering the\nrows of `CONFIGS.md` in the ranges 88-110 and 207-253, driven through the JS\nlayer (`diff_dostring` / `diff_eval`) AND through the low-level C entry points\nwhere they exist:\n  - js_newregexp with all 8 combinations of JS_REGEXP_G|JS_REGEXP_I|JS_REGEXP_M,\n    then RegExp.prototype.exec/test/toString and .source/.global/.ignoreCase/\n    .multiline/.lastIndex. Assert the lastIndex progression across repeated\n    exec() calls, with and without the global flag, and after manually assigning\n    lastIndex (including to a negative, huge, non-integer and non-numeric value).\n    Also call the exported `js_RegExp_prototype_exec` path via JS.\n  - String.prototype.split with a string separator, a regexp separator, no\n    separator, an empty separator, and with limit absent / 0 / small / huge /\n    negative / non-numeric.\n  - String.prototype.replace with a string pattern and a regexp pattern\n    (global and non-global), and a string replacement containing $$, $&, $`,\n    $', $1..$9, $10..$99 and a trailing bare $, AND a function replacement.\n    NOTE: jsstring.c:605 has `if (x > 10)` where the ES spec wants `x >= 10`;\n    reproduce whatever the C does, do not \"fix\" it.\n  - String.prototype.match / search / indexOf / lastIndexOf / slice / substring /\n    substr / charAt / charCodeAt / concat / toUpperCase / toLowerCase / trim /\n    localeCompare, over randomized ASCII AND non-ASCII (multi-byte UTF-8)\n    strings and over out-of-range and negative indices.\n  - String.fromCharCode with 0, 1, many args and with out-of-range code points.\n  - Array.prototype: sort with and without a comparator (and with a comparator\n    that returns nonsense / throws), join, concat, slice, splice, push, pop,\n    shift, unshift, reverse, indexOf, lastIndexOf, every, some, forEach, map,\n    filter, reduce, reduceRight, toString. Use dense arrays, SPARSE arrays\n    (created via `delete a[i]` and via `a.length = n`), arrays with a\n    non-index property, and empty/one/many element arrays -- this exercises the\n    flat-vs-unflattened array representation in jsrun.c/jsvalue.c.\n  - JSON.stringify with replacer absent / function / array, and space absent /\n    number 0..12 / string / long string, over nested objects and arrays and\n    every value type (including cyclic structures, undefined, functions, NaN,\n    Infinity, -0, Date objects, wrapper objects, and strings needing escaping).\n  - JSON.parse with and without a reviver, over every JSON value shape.\n  - Date: `new Date()` is NOT deterministic -- avoid it, or only compare\n    structure. DO test `new Date(ms)`, `new Date(str)` and\n    `new Date(y,m,d,h,mi,s,ms)` with 1..7 args including out-of-range and\n    fractional components; getTime/getFullYear/getMonth/getDate/getDay/getHours/\n    getMinutes/getSeconds/getMilliseconds/getTimezoneOffset and all the UTC\n    variants; setTime/setFullYear/.../setUTCHours; toString/toISOString/\n    toUTCString/toDateString/toTimeString/toJSON/valueOf; Date.parse and\n    Date.UTC. Include the invalid-date path (NaN time) for every getter and\n    formatter. NOTE: jsdate.c:748 Dp_setUTCHours defaults the minutes argument\n    from HourFromTime(t) rather than MinFromTime(t) -- that is the C's\n    behaviour and the Rust MUST match it.\n    IMPORTANT: local-timezone functions depend on the TZ environment variable;\n    both libraries run in the same process so they see the same TZ. That is\n    fine. Do not set TZ per-library.\n  - Math: every method over randomized doubles including inf/nan/-0 and the\n    exact boundary values, plus Math.random() must NOT be compared for value\n    (only that it stays in [0,1)).\n  - Number.prototype.toFixed / toExponential / toPrecision / toString(radix)\n    over randomized doubles and every digit count 0..21 and radix 2..36.\n\nRULES\n  - Every call must go through the harness (through the .so exports).\n    Never call Rust functions directly.\n  - Use MANY randomized inputs per row with a FIXED seed, not one hand-picked\n    value. Build helper functions that generate value/argument matrices.\n  - Compare byte-for-byte: return codes, output text, error messages, js_gettop.\n  - Some C paths are UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR (out-of-bounds writes, integer overflow\n    in a size computation). If you find one, do NOT test it; add a `///` comment\n    naming the file:line and why. Check the C source before assuming.\n  - Anything that recurses deeply (deep JSON nesting, deep array nesting,\n    recursive JS functions) MUST be wrapped:\n    `#[test] fn t_x(){ with_big_stack(body_t_x); } fn body_t_x(){...}`\n  - Keep each individual test under ~60s. Run with\n    `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features --test js_builtins`.\n\nIF YOU FIND A DIVERGENCE\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix `src/*.rs`. Use the Edit tool for surgical changes --\ndo NOT use Write on any existing file under src/ (another agent may be editing a\ndifferent function in the same file). Re-read the corresponding C function and\nmake the Rust match exactly. Report every divergence found and fixed.\n\nFINISH ONLY WHEN `timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features --test js_builtins`\npasses with 0 failures. Report: number of tests, CONFIGS rows covered, and every\ndivergence found+fixed (with file:line). If you could not make something pass,\nsay so explicitly rather than deleting the test.","agentId":"a9f3c7aa379af7bd5","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All done. Final verification passes.\n\n## Result\n\n`timeout 600 cargo test --no-default-features --test js_builtins` → **35 tests, 0 failures, ~5 s** (also verified 3× consecutively and under `--test-threads=1`). The whole repo suite still passes: 114 tests across 7 binaries (`api_state` 42, `js_builtins` 35, `ll_lex` 8, `ll_num` 10, `ll_regexp` 8, `ll_utf` 7, `smoke` 4).\n\nNew file: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust/tests/js_builtins.rs` (~2700 lines, ~110k generated JS statements from fixed seeds). Nothing under `src/` or `tests/common/` was touched.\n\n## Divergences found and fixed\n\n**None.** The Rust translation matched the C byte-for-byte (return code, output text, error messages, `js_gettop`, result repr) on every case, including the C quirks the task called out, which I verified are actually reached:\n- `jsstring.c:605` `if (x > 10)` → `'ab'.replace(/(a)/,'$10')` yields `\"$:b\"` in both.\n- `jsdate.c:748` `Dp_setUTCHours` defaulting minutes from `HourFromTime` → `new Date(7h33m).setUTCHours(5)` gives `05:07:00` in both.\n- `js_Regexp::last` is `unsigned short` → `lastIndex = 70000` reads back `4464`, `-1` reads back `65535`.\n- `Math.round(-0.3)` returns **+0** (C's `return -0` is an int constant).\n- `String.fromCharCode(0)` → overlong NUL, length 1; `fromCharCode(0x80000000)` → length 0.\n\n## CONFIGS.md rows covered\n\n90, 98, 116, 118–121, 152, 285–306, 404–450. **Note:** the task's stated ranges don't match the current `CONFIGS.md` — rows 88–110 there are `js_get/set/defproperty` and 207–253 are raw `js_regcomp` shapes (already covered by `ll_regexp.rs`). I covered the rows matching the task's *content* description, listed in the file's module header.\n\n## C undefined behaviour found and excluded (documented with `///`)\n\n- **`c_src/src/jsstring.c:573`** — `Sp_replace_regexp`'s *function*-replacement arm does `for (x = 0; m.sub[x].sp; ++x)`, relying on a NULL terminator that only exists because `regexec` clears all `REG_MAXSUB`==16 slots. A regexp with the maximum 15 capture groups where **all** participate fills `sub[0..15]`, so it reads `m.sub[16]` — past the end of the stack-local `Resub` — and pushes a garbage pointer. **Both `libmujs.so` builds SIGSEGV on it** (I hit it, isolated it to each library separately, then capped that variant at 13 groups). All other capture walks (`Sp_split_regexp`, `js_RegExp_prototype_exec`, the string-replacement arm) are bounded by `m.nsub` and are tested up to 15 groups.\n- **`c_src/src/jsarray.c:279`, `:365`** — `Ap_sort_cmp`/`Ap_sort_swap` read `u.a.simple`/`u.a.array` without checking the object class, so `Array.prototype.sort.call(nonArray, …)` reads a never-stored union member.\n- **`c_src/src/jsstring.c:253-258`** — `Sp_substring_imp`'s `js_try` handler frees `p` before it is assigned (OOM-only).\n- **`c_src/src/jsdate.c:319-347`** — `fmtdate`/`fmttime` convert a NaN time to `int` and overflow `365*(y-1970)` *before* the `isfinite` guard; results are discarded and `d2i` in `src/jsi.rs` already reproduces gcc's `cvttsd2si`, so the invalid-date path is tested.\n\n## Two things worth flagging\n\n1. **The batch helper is hardened against vacuous passes.** My first version had 4 tests whose generated scripts contained syntax errors (e.g. `.split(, 0)`, `.slice(1, )`, `JSON.parse(''a'')`); both libraries reported the identical syntax error, so the batches \"passed\" without executing anything. `diff_lines_n` now asserts `load_rc == 0`, `call_rc == 0` and a floor on emitted output lines, which surfaced all four; they are fixed.\n2. **TZ is pinned once, process-wide** (`XYZ5:30`, offset +330 min) inside a `OnceLock` before either library caches `LocalTZA`, with an assertion that both libraries agree. Without it a UTC host leaves `LocalTime()`/`UTC()`/`getTimezoneOffset` and `fmttime`'s signed-offset arm completely untested. `fmttime`'s `tza > 0` arm is unreachable from a single process (the only non-zero `tza` any caller passes is the once-cached `LocalTZA()`), which is noted in the file. The warm-up also removes a pre-existing latent race: `LocalTZA` uses `gmtime`/`localtime`, which share one static `struct tm` in libc, so two parallel tests seeding the two libraries' caches could have produced different offsets."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":2226053,"totalTokens":329759,"totalToolUseCount":106,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2676,"cache_read_input_tokens":324818,"output_tokens":2263,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":412},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2676},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":21,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":71,"editFileCount":14,"linesAdded":2349,"linesRemoved":132,"otherToolCount":0}}}
