d call; includes a callable rhs whose `prototype` is `1` and `Function.prototype` (callable, no `prototype` property) for row 181's two typeerrors.\n\n## Divergences found\n\n**None.** The Rust translation matched the C byte-for-byte on every case.\n\nTo prove the tests are not vacuous I temporarily injected three mutations into `src/jsrun.rs` (`js_pushboolean` dropping `!!v`; `js_pushstring`'s SHRSTR threshold `<=` → `<`; a wrong source slot in `js_rot3`) and confirmed 5 tests failed. All three were reverted with surgical edits; `src/jsrun.rs` is byte-identical to its pre-mutation content and `target/debug/.src_hash` is unchanged at `33ecfc68023115a1` (same value as the first build of the session). `tests/smoke.rs` also still passes. **No `src/*.rs` changes were needed or kept.**\n\n## Deliberately excluded (C undefined behaviour, documented in the file header with file:line)\n\n`js_pop` with `n<0` (jsrun.c:403, raises TOP into uninitialised slots); the whole unchecked `js_dup`/`js_dup2`/`js_rot2-4`/`js_rot2pop1`/`js_rot3pop2` family and `js_rot(n>gettop)` below their value requirement (jsrun.c:442-498, indexes before the array start when BOT==0); indices near `INT_MIN` (jsrun.c:220 signed overflow); `js_pushlstring` with `n<0` or `n==JS_STRLIMIT` (jsrun.c:163); `js_isuserdata(..., NULL)` over an actual userdata (jsrun.c:266 `strcmp(NULL,...)`); and `js_currentfunctiondata` with a JS_CFUNCTION at BOT-1 (jsrun.c:211 — noted as unreachable through the public API rather than UB, since jsproperty.c:167 memsets the object)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a8a7968983ed02fe4 (use SendMessage with to: 'a8a7968983ed02fe4', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 288407\ntool_uses: 90\nduration_ms: 1787231</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2","uuid":"40c00c3d-d035-4106-871e-f9f737a62e54","timestamp":"2026-08-23T11:05:57.157Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Work in /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust\n\nCONTEXT\nC->Rust translation of MuJS, verified by DIFFERENTIAL TESTING against the C.\n`c_src/` is the ground truth: READ-ONLY, never modify it and never create any\nfile under it. `src/*.rs` is the Rust translation built as a cdylib.\n\nCRITICAL BUILD RULE\n`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib (the tests reach it only via dlopen).\nALWAYS run `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test api_stack` -- that script builds\nthe cdylib, stamps a source hash, then runs cargo test. If you run bare\n`cargo test` after editing src/, the harness will abort with a \"STALE Rust\ncdylib\" assertion. Never bypass that assertion.\n\nREAD FIRST\n  tests/common/mod.rs           - the harness (~1350 lines)\n  tests/api_state.rs            - a large existing example in exactly the style wanted\n  tests/gaps.rs                 - shows protected-call probes + stdout capture\n  CONFIGS.md                    - the configuration-surface table\n\nHARNESS API\n  libs() -> &'static Pair { c: Lib, rs: Lib }\n  Lib has an `unsafe fn` per exported symbol; plus generic helpers:\n      l.pred(\"js_isarray\", j, idx) -> c_int          // (JS,int)->int\n      l.nullary(\"js_dup\", j) / l.nullary_i(\"js_equal\", j)\n      l.raw2::<unsafe extern \"C\" fn(...)>(\"name\")    // arbitrary signature\n  new_state(l, flags) -> JS      // installs report hook + `print`/`dump` globals\n  set_cur(l)                     // call before entering a library (callbacks)\n  eval / dostring / diff_eval / diff_dostring\n  cstr / from_c / out_clear / out_take\n  Rng::new(seed): next_u32/below/range/f64_sane/f64_any/ascii_string/\n                  unicode_string/raw_bytes\n  with_big_stack(fn_item)        // 512MB stack for deep recursion\n\nYOUR TASK\nCreate ONE new file `tests/api_stack.rs` covering CONFIGS.md rows 41-70 and\n142-182, i.e. the stack API, the value pushes, and every conversion /\ncomparison. Specifically:\n\n1. STACK API: js_gettop, js_pop, js_rot, js_copy, js_remove, js_insert,\n   js_replace, js_dup, js_dup2, js_rot2, js_rot3, js_rot4, js_rot2pop1,\n   js_rot3pop2. Drive them as RANDOMISED OPERATION SEQUENCES: build a stack of\n   known values, apply a random sequence of 20-80 operations with random\n   (positive AND negative, in-range AND out-of-range) indices, and after EVERY\n   step compare the ENTIRE observable stack contents (js_gettop plus js_type /\n   js_typeof / js_tryrepr at every index) between C and Rust. Use a fixed seed\n   and many sequences.\n   IMPORTANT: `js_insert` is unconditionally `js_error(J, \"not implemented yet\")`\n   (jsrun.c:424) -- verify BOTH libraries throw the identical error, and note\n   that it must be called inside a protected call.\n   IMPORTANT: read `stackidx` in jsrun.c first to learn exactly which indices are\n   valid and what happens for out-of-range ones; some out-of-range values are\n   UB (reads outside the stack array). Test only the well-defined ones and add a\n   `///` comment for the rest naming file:line.\n\n2. PUSHES + STRING REPRESENTATION: js_pushundefined/null/boolean/number/string/\n   lstring/literal/global, js_newobject/newobjectx/newarray/newboolean/\n   newnumber/newstring. Cover the JS_TSHRSTR (n <= 15 inline) vs JS_TLITSTR vs\n   JS_TMEMSTR axis: read jsrun.c js_pushstring/js_pushlstring/js_pushliteral and\n   jsvalue.c js_newstring to find the exact length thresholds (they differ!), and\n   test lengths on both sides of each. Include embedded NUL bytes and n = 0.\n   Also push booleans with values other than 0/1 (js_pushboolean takes any int).\n\n3. CONVERSIONS over EVERY value shape: js_toboolean, js_tonumber, js_tostring,\n   js_tointeger, js_toint32, js_touint32, js_toint16, js_touint16, js_typeof,\n   js_type, js_repr, js_torepr, and the try* variants js_trystring/js_trynumber/\n   js_tryinteger/js_tryboolean/js_tryrepr. The value shapes must include:\n   undefined, null, true, false, +0, -0, NaN, +-Infinity, integers, fractions,\n   huge/subnormal doubles, short strings, long strings, numeric strings,\n   plain objects, arrays (empty/dense/sparse), functions, C functions, userdata,\n   regexps, Dates, and Number/String/Boolean wrapper objects. Build the values by\n   evaluating a JS expression and leaving the result on the stack, then call each\n   conversion at that index. Randomise the doubles and strings with a fixed seed.\n   NOTE: js_tostring on a number and js_toobject on a primitive MUTATE the stack\n   slot in place -- assert that the post-call stack state matches too.\n   NOTE: the try* variants return a default on throw; construct throwing values\n   (e.g. `{toString:function(){throw 1}}`) and check both return the default.\n\n4. PREDICATES: js_isdefined/isundefined/isnull/isboolean/isnumber/isstring/\n   isprimitive/isobject/isarray/isregexp/iscoercible/iscallable/iserror/\n   isnumberobject/isstringobject/isbooleanobject/isdateobject, and\n   js_isuserdata with a matching tag, a non-matching tag, and a NULL tag, over\n   the same full set of value shapes AND at out-of-range indices.\n\n5. COMPARISONS: js_compare (check the `okay` out-parameter too), js_equal,\n   js_strictequal, js_instanceof, js_concat over the CROSS PRODUCT of the value\n   shapes (at least ~40x40). js_instanceof throws for a non-callable right-hand\n   side -- do it in a protected call.\n\n6. js_currentfunction / js_currentfunctiondata at top level (BOT == 0) and\n   inside a C function.\n\nRULES\n  - Every call must go through the harness (i.e. through the .so exports).\n    Never call Rust functions directly.\n  - MANY randomized inputs per row, fixed seed. Not one hand-picked value.\n  - Compare byte-for-byte, including js_gettop after every operation.\n  - NEVER call a throwing entry point outside a protected context\n    (js_pcall/js_pconstruct/js_ploadstring/js_dostring). It longjmps out of your\n    test and corrupts the process. Use the cfunction-probe pattern from\n    tests/gaps.rs (`newregexp_probe`) for anything that can throw.\n  - Some C paths are UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR. Do NOT test those; add a `///` comment\n    naming file:line and why. Check the C source before assuming.\n  - Keep each test under ~60s.\n\nIF YOU FIND A DIVERGENCE\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix `src/*.rs` with the Edit tool (surgical edits only --\ndo NOT use Write on any existing file under src/, another agent may be editing a\ndifferent function in the same file). Re-read the C function and make the Rust\nmatch exactly.\n\nFINISH ONLY WHEN `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test api_stack` passes with 0\nfailures, run 2x to confirm it is not flaky. Report: number of tests, CONFIGS\nrows covered, and every divergence found+fixed with file:line. If something\ncannot pass, say so explicitly rather than deleting the test.","agentId":"a8a7968983ed02fe4","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test api_stack` passes with **13 tests, 0 failures**, confirmed on two consecutive runs (~2.1s each).\n\n## What was created\n\nOne new file: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust/tests/api_stack.rs` (1644 lines, 13 tests). Every call goes through the `.so` exports via the harness; no Rust function is called directly.\n\nInfrastructure worth noting:\n- `diff2` runs a closure against both libs and compares the full transcript, reporting a window around the **first differing line** (transcripts run to megabytes).\n- `stack_snap` / `snap_at` dump the *entire* observable stack: `js_gettop` plus `js_type`/`js_typeof`/predicates/value bits/`js_tryrepr` at every positive index, every negative index, and a 7-index out-of-range ring. `js_tryrepr` is applied to a `js_copy` because `js_torepr` ends in `js_replace(idx<0?idx-1:idx)` (jsrepr.c:271) and would rewrite the slot.\n- `strtag` separates **JS_TSHRSTR / JS_TLITSTR / JS_TMEMSTR** without reading the tag: `jsV_tostring` returns a pointer *into the stack slot* for SHRSTR, the caller's own pointer for LITSTR, and the `js_String` payload for MEMSTR, so a `js_copy` changes the pointer only for SHRSTR. Cross-checked against the `js_gc(J,1)` string counts.\n- Value shapes (152 for conversions/predicates, 48 for the cross product) are parked in the registry so any frame can re-push the *exact* `js_Value`, tag included. They cover every `js_Type` and every `js_Class` including JS_CSCRIPT (via `js_ploadstring` without calling), JS_CITERATOR, JS_CARGUMENTS, JS_CUSERDATA, JS_CMATH, JS_CJSON.\n\n## CONFIGS rows covered\n\n- **41-54** — `t_stack_ops_random` (120 seeded sequences × 20-80 ops, whole stack compared after **every** step; ops chosen from the *legal* set so rows 51/52/53 hit their exact 2/3/4-value boundary), `t_stack_errors`, `t_frames_and_currentfunction`.\n- **43/47/49** — `t_stack_errors`: 19 indices × 3 frame shapes × `js_pop`/`js_remove`/`js_replace`/`js_copy`/`js_rot`, all inside `js_pcall` so BOT>0 makes the `idx < BOT` arm reachable.\n- **50** — `t_insert_never_implemented`: 44 (idx, stack-shape) combos, all `(new Error(\"not implemented yet\"))` in both libs, always in a protected call.\n- **55** — `t_push_limits`: CHECKSTACK(1) via `js_pushnumber`/`js_copy`/`js_dup` and CHECKSTACK(2) via `js_dup2`.\n- **56** — `t_frames_and_currentfunction`: `js_currentfunction`/`js_currentfunctiondata` at BOT==0, in nested frames (depth 0/1/3), with `js_newcfunction` (data NULL) and `js_newcfunctionx` (same `data` pointer passed to both libs so equality is comparable), plus via `js_dostring`.\n- **57-70 (+71-73)** — `t_pushes_and_strings` (300+ bodies incl. lengths 0-20/30-32/63-65, embedded NULs, random ascii/unicode/raw bytes) and `t_push_values_and_new` (774 doubles, `js_pushboolean` over INT_MIN/INT_MAX/64 random ints, all 38 native builders).\n- **142-158** — `t_conversions`: 152 shapes × 22 entry points × {0, JS_STRICT}, pre/post slot state recorded (catches `js_tostring`-on-number and `js_toobject`-on-primitive in-place mutation).\n- **159** — `t_predicates`: 17 predicates + `js_isuserdata`×4 tags over 152 shapes at 311 indices.\n- **160-162** — `t_conversions` + `t_try_conversions_at_limit` (nested `js_pcall` depths 0/1/60-64/66 straddling JS_TRYLIMIT).\n- **163** — `js_touserdata` (right tag / wrong tag / non-userdata) and `js_toregexp` in `t_conversions`.\n- **164-168** — `t_repr_family`: `js_repr`/`js_torepr`/`js_tryrepr` at 11 indices × 3 entry points showing exactly which slot each touches, plus cycles, holes, errors with/without `message`, and escaping.\n- **169-182** — `t_comparisons`: 48×48 cross product × {`js_compare` (with the `okay` out-param), `js_equal`, `js_strictequal`, `js_instanceof`, `js_concat`} × 2 flag settings, each in its own protected call; includes a callable rhs whose `prototype` is `1` and `Function.prototype` (callable, no `prototype` property) for row 181's two typeerrors.\n\n## Divergences found\n\n**None.** The Rust translation matched the C byte-for-byte on every case.\n\nTo prove the tests are not vacuous I temporarily injected three mutations into `src/jsrun.rs` (`js_pushboolean` dropping `!!v`; `js_pushstring`'s SHRSTR threshold `<=` → `<`; a wrong source slot in `js_rot3`) and confirmed 5 tests failed. All three were reverted with surgical edits; `src/jsrun.rs` is byte-identical to its pre-mutation content and `target/debug/.src_hash` is unchanged at `33ecfc68023115a1` (same value as the first build of the session). `tests/smoke.rs` also still passes. **No `src/*.rs` changes were needed or kept.**\n\n## Deliberately excluded (C undefined behaviour, documented in the file header with file:line)\n\n`js_pop` with `n<0` (jsrun.c:403, raises TOP into uninitialised slots); the whole unchecked `js_dup`/`js_dup2`/`js_rot2-4`/`js_rot2pop1`/`js_rot3pop2` family and `js_rot(n>gettop)` below their value requirement (jsrun.c:442-498, indexes before the array start when BOT==0); indices near `INT_MIN` (jsrun.c:220 signed overflow); `js_pushlstring` with `n<0` or `n==JS_STRLIMIT` (jsrun.c:163); `js_isuserdata(..., NULL)` over an actual userdata (jsrun.c:266 `strcmp(NULL,...)`); and `js_currentfunctiondata` with a JS_CFUNCTION at BOT-1 (jsrun.c:211 — noted as unreachable through the public API rather than UB, since jsproperty.c:167 memsets the object)."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":1787231,"totalTokens":288407,"totalToolUseCount":90,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":550,"cache_read_input_tokens":285425,"output_tokens":2430,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":550},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":1,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":70,"editFileCount":19,"linesAdded":3277,"linesRemoved":73,"otherToolCount":0}}}
