-0eE2Fw/translated_rust\n\nCONTEXT\nC->Rust translation of MuJS, verified by DIFFERENTIAL TESTING against the C.\n`c_src/` is the ground truth: READ-ONLY, never modify it and never create any\nfile under it. `src/*.rs` is the Rust translation built as a cdylib.\n\nCRITICAL BUILD RULE\n`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib (the tests reach it only via dlopen).\nALWAYS run `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test api_props` -- that script builds\nthe cdylib, stamps a source hash, then runs cargo test. If you run bare\n`cargo test` after editing src/, the harness aborts with a \"STALE Rust cdylib\"\nassertion. Never bypass that assertion.\n\nREAD FIRST\n  tests/common/mod.rs   - the harness (~1350 lines)\n  tests/api_state.rs    - a large existing example in exactly the style wanted\n  tests/gaps.rs         - shows protected-call probes\n  CONFIGS.md            - the configuration-surface table\n\nHARNESS API\n  libs() -> &'static Pair { c: Lib, rs: Lib }\n  Lib has an `unsafe fn` per exported symbol; plus generic helpers:\n      l.pred(\"js_isarray\", j, idx) -> c_int\n      l.nullary(\"js_dup\", j) / l.nullary_i(\"js_equal\", j)\n      l.raw2::<unsafe extern \"C\" fn(...)>(\"name\")\n  new_state(l, flags) -> JS      // installs report hook + `print`/`dump` globals\n  set_cur(l)                     // call before entering a library (callbacks)\n  eval / dostring / diff_eval / diff_dostring\n  cstr / from_c / out_clear / out_take\n  Rng::new(seed): next_u32/below/range/f64_sane/ascii_string/unicode_string\n  with_big_stack(fn_item)        // 512MB stack for deep recursion\n\nYOUR TASK\nCreate ONE new file `tests/api_props.rs` covering CONFIGS.md rows 71-141:\nobject creation, the property API with its attribute cross-product, the ARRAY\nREPRESENTATION axis, and iterators.\n\n1. OBJECT CREATION: js_newobject, js_newobjectx, js_newarray, js_newboolean,\n   js_newnumber, js_newstring, js_newcfunction, js_newcfunctionx (with a data\n   pointer and a finalizer), js_newcconstructor, js_newuserdata,\n   js_newuserdatax (all of has/put/del/finalize hooks). For each, verify the\n   resulting object through js_type / js_typeof / js_torepr /\n   Object.prototype.toString / the full predicate set, and that it behaves\n   identically when used from JS.\n   For js_newuserdatax record every hook invocation (name + arguments) into a\n   transcript and assert the transcripts are identical between C and Rust --\n   the ORDER and COUNT of hook calls matters.\n\n2. PROPERTY API x ATTRIBUTES: js_hasproperty, js_getproperty, js_setproperty,\n   js_defproperty, js_delproperty, js_defaccessor, js_getlength, js_setlength,\n   js_hasindex, js_getindex, js_setindex, js_delindex.\n   Drive the FULL cross-product of JS_READONLY | JS_DONTENUM | JS_DONTCONF\n   (all 8 combinations) x receiver class (plain object, array, string object,\n   number object, boolean object, function, C function, regexp, Date, userdata,\n   arguments object, the global object) x strict and non-strict mode.\n   Read jsrun.c jsR_getproperty / jsR_setproperty / jsR_defproperty /\n   jsR_delproperty first: every `->strict` branch is a silent no-op in sloppy\n   mode and a TypeError in strict mode; cover BOTH sides of each.\n   Also cover the per-class special property names the C special-cases:\n   \"length\" on arrays / strings / functions, \"lastIndex\"/\"source\"/\"global\"/\n   \"ignoreCase\"/\"multiline\" on regexps, \"callee\"/\"length\" on arguments, and\n   index-like names on string objects. NOTE jsrun.c:840 vs :749 -- defproperty\n   refuses regexp `lastIndex` while setproperty allows it; assert that asymmetry.\n   Use RANDOMISED property names (including numeric-looking ones, very long ones,\n   ones with embedded high bytes) and randomised values, fixed seed.\n\n3. ARRAY REPRESENTATION (the most important part): arrays have TWO internal\n   representations, a flat/`simple` one and an unflattened hashed property tree,\n   and `jsR_unflattenarray` / `jsV_resizearray` convert between them. Read\n   jsR_setarrayindex, jsR_unflattenarray, jsV_resizearray and every use of\n   `u.a.simple` / `flat_length` / `flat_capacity` in jsrun.c and jsvalue.c.\n   Then drive RANDOMISED SEQUENCES of array mutations -- dense append, write at\n   a gap, write at flat_length exactly, delete the last element, delete a middle\n   element, shrink length, grow length, add a non-index property, defproperty,\n   defaccessor, Object.seal/freeze/preventExtensions, sort, splice, reverse --\n   and after EVERY step compare the fully observable array state between C and\n   Rust: length, every element via js_getindex, js_hasindex, the `in` operator,\n   Object.keys order, for-in enumeration order, JSON.stringify, and js_torepr.\n   Use at least a few hundred randomised sequences with a fixed seed. This is\n   where representation-transition bugs hide.\n   NOTE jsrun.c:673-678 has three `assert()`s in jsR_setarrayindex; the C is\n   built WITHOUT -DNDEBUG so they are live and abort() on failure. Do not try to\n   violate them.\n\n4. ITERATORS: js_pushiterator(J, idx, own) with own = 0 and 1, and\n   js_nextiterator, over: plain objects, objects with shadowed prototype-chain\n   properties, objects with DONTENUM properties, arrays in BOTH representations,\n   sparse arrays, string objects, empty objects, and objects mutated DURING\n   iteration. Compare the full sequence of names returned, in order.\n   Also compare against JS-level `for (k in o)`.\n\nRULES\n  - Every call must go through the harness (through the .so exports). Never call\n    Rust functions directly.\n  - MANY randomized inputs per row, fixed seed. Not one hand-picked value.\n  - Compare byte-for-byte, including js_gettop after every operation.\n  - NEVER call a throwing entry point outside a protected context\n    (js_pcall/js_pconstruct/js_ploadstring/js_dostring). Use the cfunction-probe\n    pattern from tests/gaps.rs (`newregexp_probe`).\n  - Some C paths are UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR. Do NOT test those; add a `///` comment\n    naming file:line and why. Check the C source before assuming.\n  - Keep each test under ~60s.\n\nIF YOU FIND A DIVERGENCE\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix `src/*.rs` with the Edit tool (surgical edits only --\ndo NOT use Write on any existing file under src/, another agent may be editing a\ndifferent function in the same file). Re-read the C function and make the Rust\nmatch exactly.\n\nFINISH ONLY WHEN `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test api_props` passes with 0\nfailures, run 2x to confirm it is not flaky. Report: number of tests, CONFIGS\nrows covered, and every divergence found+fixed with file:line. If something\ncannot pass, say so explicitly rather than deleting the test.","agentId":"ad9fffb05bfab2455","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test api_props` passes with **0 failures**, confirmed on repeated runs (3x), and the whole suite (`./run_tests.sh`, 168 tests) still passes.\n\n## What was created\n\n`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust/tests/api_props.rs` — **25 tests, 2958 lines**, ~8s wall clock.\n\nEverything goes through the two `.so` exports. Every throwing entry point runs inside a `cf_op` / `cf_csnap` cfunction invoked with `js_pcall` (the `gaps.rs` probe pattern). After every operation the transcript records `js_gettop` before/after, the `js_pcall` rc, and the full observable state through **both** the C API (`js_getlength`, `js_hasindex`/`js_getindex` over -1..15, `js_pushiterator` own=0 and own=1 drained with `js_nextiterator`, `js_torepr`) **and** JS (`length`, `for-in` order, `Object.keys` order, `Object.getOwnPropertyNames`, `in`, element reads, `JSON.stringify`, `Object.prototype.toString`, `isExtensible/isSealed/isFrozen`).\n\n## CONFIGS rows covered: 71-141 (all 71 rows)\n\n- **71-85 object creation**: 31 target classes × strict/sloppy; `js_newobjectx` over every value tag; `js_newstring` at the 15/16 boundary and every UTF-8 boundary rune; `js_newnumber`/`js_newboolean` over 100+ randomised doubles/ints; `js_newcfunction[x]`/`js_newcconstructor` × length × finalizer (finalizer count asserted); `js_newuserdatax` with all 16 hook masks and a **full hook transcript** (name + data arg, order and count asserted per op); `js_isuserdata`/`js_touserdata` tag matrix.\n- **86-117 property API × attributes**: full 8-way `JS_READONLY|JS_DONTENUM|JS_DONTCONF` cross-product × 31 receiver classes × strict/sloppy; ~300 randomised names (numeric-looking, 300-byte, raw high bytes, UTF-8 surrogates) × randomised values × positive/negative stack index; the per-class special names; 8×8 `js_defaccessor` operand matrix; prototype-chain getter/setter/readonly/shadowing; `js_getlength`/`js_setlength` incl. the negative-`idx` `idx-1` compensation.\n- **118-132 array representation**: 14 targeted flat↔hashed transition cases plus **900 randomised sequences × 17 steps** with a full state comparison after every step (with marker assertions that the walk actually reached both representations, sealing, readonly, invalid-length and accessor paths), and the reachable-simple-array GC half of row 132.\n- **133-141 iterators**: 15 classes × 11 setups (incl. enumerable vs `DONTENUM` prototype shadowing, two-level chains), both `own` values, delete-during-iteration, the `J->scratch` aliasing, non-iterator `js_nextiterator`, iterator-survives-GC, plus **320 randomised objects compared against JS `for (k in o)`**.\n- A generic **700-sequence × 13-step random walk over the whole property API on every class**.\n\n## Divergences found and fixed (all in `src/jsrun.rs`)\n\nAll three are C *unspecified argument evaluation order* that gcc x86-64 fixes right-to-left; verified against `objdump -d c_src/build/libmujs.so` and each fix proven necessary by reverting it and watching tests fail.\n\n1. **`src/jsrun.rs:1257` `js_setproperty`** (C: `jsrun.c:1011`). C evaluates `!js_isobject(J, idx)` *before* `js_toobject(J, idx)` rewrites the slot, so a primitive receiver is `transient`. Rust evaluated left-to-right → `transient=0`, silently creating properties on the throwaway wrapper instead of the strict `\"cannot create property 'x' on transient object\"` typeerror / sloppy no-op. Caught by `t_transient_receiver`, `t_attribute_crossproduct`, `t_random_property_names`, `t_length_and_index_api`, `t_array_flat_transitions`, `t_array_representation_random`.\n2. **`src/jsrun.rs:1341` `js_setindex`** (C: `jsrun.c:1049`). Same defect, same fix. Caught by 4 tests.\n3. **`src/jsrun.rs:1296` `js_defaccessor`** (C: `jsrun.c:1028`). C resolves `jsR_tofunction(J,-1)` (setter), then `(J,-2)` (getter), then `js_toobject(J, idx)`. Rust did `js_toobject` first, so a non-coercible receiver plus a non-callable accessor reported `\"cannot convert undefined to object\"` where C reports `\"not a function\"`. Caught by `t_defaccessor_matrix`.\n\n## Paths deliberately not driven (documented in the file header with file:line)\n\n- **`jsrun.c:551-552`** — the second half of row 132. `jsR_unflattenarray`'s `js_try` handler sets `obj->properties = NULL`, which `jsproperty.c:48` (`lookup`), `jsgc.c:101` (`jsG_scanobject`) and `jsgc.c:35` (`jsG_freeobject`) then dereference unconditionally. Reaching it makes *any* later property access, `js_gc` or `js_freestate` a NULL dereference — undefined behaviour, unobservable in-process. The reachable-simple-array GC half is driven.\n- **`jsrun.c:673-678`** — the three live `assert()`s in `jsR_setarrayindex` (`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no `-DNDEBUG`). Both callers pre-check `simple && 0 <= k <= flat_length`, so they cannot be violated via the public API; no test tries.\n- **`jsvalue.c:539`** — `js_newuserdatax`'s finalizer-on-construction-failure needs a failing allocator; already covered by `api_state.rs::t_finalizer_on_construction_failure`, not duplicated.\n\nTwo bounded-work concessions (both keyed on the identical `js_getlength`, so symmetric): `jsrepr.c:125` `reprarray` and `json.c:300