pill (10 pointers / 12 ints), SSE spill (10-12 doubles), interleaved GP+SSE spill, `%ld`/`%lld`, `%.*s`/`%*d`, `NULL %s` → `(null)`, and 255/256-byte truncation.\n- Row 81 (`jsR_defproperty`'s readonly label with neither `strict` nor `throw`) is only reachable through `js_initvar` against a `with` scope object — `with([1,2,3]) { eval('var length') }` silently drops the declaration in both libraries.\n- Row 125 (`OP_GETLOCAL`'s non-lightweight ReferenceError) needs the Bug-701886 skip in `jsR_callscript`: `this.b = 1; eval('var b; delete b; b')`.\n- `js_pushvalue`'s 16-byte `js_Value` really is classified INTEGER,INTEGER (confirmed by `objdump`: it reads `%rsi`/`%rdx`), so it is called across the FFI as `fn(JS, u64, u64)`.\n- An asymmetry worth knowing: `js_tryrepr` (jsrepr.c:278) installs its handler with a bare `js_try` and **no** `js_ptry` guard, unlike `js_trystring` (jsstate.c:50) — so at `trytop == JS_TRYLIMIT` it throws instead of returning the default. `vshape_notry` exists in the test file for exactly that reason.\n- `js_pconstruct` computes `savetop = TOP - n - 2` while `js_construct` wants the callee at `-n-1`, so its error path writes one slot *below* the callee; `t_call_paths` keeps two guard slots underneath so the write stays in memory we own (with an empty stack it would be `STACK[-1]`, so that is not tested).\n\nI deliberately did **not** touch `ERRORS.md`'s `cov` column — sibling agents own rows 259-933 of the same file and concurrent writes would race; the TSV is the merge input."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a53a5768ccd537970 (use SendMessage with to: 'a53a5768ccd537970', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 474760\ntool_uses: 175\nduration_ms: 3796437</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2","uuid":"fff01297-4ba4-4984-881b-77d51d5642e7","timestamp":"2026-08-23T12:37:02.083Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Work in /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust\n\nCONTEXT\nC->Rust translation of MuJS, verified by DIFFERENTIAL TESTING against the C.\n`c_src/` is the ground truth: READ-ONLY, never modify it and never create any\nfile under it. `src/*.rs` is the Rust translation built as a cdylib.\n\nCRITICAL BUILD RULE\n`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib (the tests reach it only via dlopen).\nALWAYS run `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test err_core`. Bare `cargo test` after\nediting src/ will trip a \"STALE Rust cdylib\" assertion. Never bypass it.\n\nREAD FIRST\n  ERRORS.md             - the error-surface table (933 rows). YOUR ROWS ARE 1-258.\n  tests/common/mod.rs   - the harness\n  tests/api_props.rs    - a large existing example, and the `cf_op` protected-call\n                          probe pattern\n  tests/gaps.rs         - protected-call probes, stdout capture, UB documentation\n                          style\n\nHARNESS API\n  libs() -> &'static Pair { c: Lib, rs: Lib }\n  Lib has an `unsafe fn` per exported symbol; plus\n      l.pred(\"js_isarray\", j, idx) -> c_int\n      l.nullary / l.nullary_i / l.newerror(\"js_newtypeerror\", j, msg)\n      l.raw2::<unsafe extern \"C\" fn(...)>(\"name\")\n  new_state(l, flags) -> JS   // installs report hook + `print`/`dump` globals\n  set_cur(l)                  // before entering a library (callbacks)\n  eval / dostring / diff_eval / diff_dostring\n  cstr / from_c / out_clear / out_take\n  Rng::new(seed)\n  with_big_stack(fn_item)     // 512MB stack, REQUIRED for deep recursion\n\nYOUR TASK: PHASE C for ERRORS.md rows 1-258\n(these are jsrun.c 1-132, jsstate.c 133-152, jserror.c 153-166,\n jsvalue.c 167-204, jsproperty.c 205-220, jsintern.c 221-230, jsgc.c 231-258)\n\nCreate ONE new file `tests/err_core.rs`. For EVERY row in that range, write a\ndifferential test that CONSTRUCTS THAT EXACT invalid input/condition, calls BOTH\nthe C and the Rust `.so`, and asserts they return the SAME error - the same error\ncode / sentinel / message text, not merely \"both failed somehow\".\n\nGroup rows into tests by mechanism, but make sure every row is actually reached.\nKey things you must cover, reading the C first:\n  - The four bare-string throws (jsrun.c:19/27/35/43): \"exception stack\n    overflow\", \"stack overflow\", \"out of memory\", \"script ran too long\". These\n    push a JS_TLITSTR, NOT an Error object, so `e instanceof Error` is false and\n    `e.message` is undefined. Assert the thrown VALUE's shape, not just that a\n    throw happened.\n  - JS_STACKSIZE 4096 (CHECKSTACK), JS_ENVLIMIT 1024 (env stack AND trace stack\n    -- note jsR_pushtrace uses `==` while jsR_savescope uses `>=`), JS_TRYLIMIT\n    64, JS_ARRAYLIMIT 1<<26, JS_STRLIMIT 1<<28.\n  - memlimit / runlimit via js_setlimit, and an allocator that fails.\n  - Every strict-vs-sloppy fork in jsR_setproperty / jsR_defproperty /\n    jsR_delproperty / js_setvar / js_delvar / jsV_setproperty / jsV_toprimitive:\n    silent no-op in sloppy mode vs TypeError/ReferenceError in strict mode. Cover\n    BOTH sides of each.\n  - jsrun.c:840 vs :749: defproperty refuses regexp `lastIndex` while\n    setproperty allows it.\n  - js_insert is unconditionally `js_error(J, \"not implemented yet\")`.\n  - jserror.c: the message TRUNCATION at 256 bytes (vsnprintf into buf[256]) for\n    all seven js_*error functions and for each stack-trace line. Drive a >255\n    byte message and assert both truncate identically. The variadic entry points\n    (js_error, js_typeerror, ...) are naked-asm trampolines in src/lib.rs -- test\n    them ACROSS THE FFI with real varargs (0, 1, several args; %s, %d, %f mixes;\n    enough args to spill past the 6 GP / 8 SSE registers onto the stack). This is\n    the highest-risk code in the translation.\n  - js_newerror / js_newevalerror / js_newrangeerror / js_newreferenceerror /\n    js_newsyntaxerror / js_newtypeerror / js_newurierror with normal, empty,\n    255/256/257-byte, and embedded-% messages.\n  - jsvalue.c conversion errors, jsproperty.c, jsintern.c, jsgc.c.\n  - jsgc.c:255: `100*gtot/ntot` is a SIGFPE when ntot==0 -- check whether that is\n    reachable from js_newstate at all before testing it; if not, say so in a\n    comment.\n\nALSO cover the generic FFI boundary abuse for these entry points even if not in\nthe table: NULL pointers where a `const char *` is expected, zero and oversized\nlengths, values one step past a documented range, and OUT-OF-RANGE ENUM VALUES\nacross the FFI (C enums accept any int, so e.g. js_newstate flags = 0xFFFF,\njs_defproperty atts = -1 / 8 / 255 / INT_MIN, js_type/js_pushiterator arguments\nwith no valid variant). Assert C and Rust agree.\n\nRULES\n  - Every call must go through the harness (through the .so exports). Never call\n    Rust functions directly.\n  - MANY randomized inputs where a row has a value dimension, fixed seed.\n  - NEVER call a throwing entry point outside a protected context\n    (js_pcall/js_pconstruct/js_ploadstring/js_dostring). Use the cfunction-probe\n    pattern. A throw with trytop==0 ends in abort() and kills the test process.\n  - Some C paths are UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR (out-of-bounds writes, negative lengths,\n    reading off the end of a buffer). Do NOT test those; add a `///` comment\n    naming file:line and why. Check the C source before assuming.\n  - Keep each test under ~60s. Deep recursion needs with_big_stack.\n\nDELIVERABLE 2: write a coverage map to\n  /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/tmp/vwork/cov_err_core.tsv\nwith one line per ERRORS.md row you covered:\n  <row number>\\t<test function name>\nOnly list rows whose test actually passes. For any row you could NOT cover, add\n  <row number>\\tUNCOVERABLE: <reason>\n\nIF YOU FIND A DIVERGENCE\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix `src/*.rs` with the Edit tool (surgical edits only --\nnever Write over an existing src file). Re-read the C and make the Rust match.\n\nFINISH ONLY WHEN `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test err_core` pa