{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aa366551246145c23","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01AYMMGss6NtEjS6jc3DmLxb","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-0eE2Fw-translated-rust/a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2/tasks/aa366551246145c23.output","summary":"Done.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n**`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust/tests/err_syntax.rs`** — 66 differential tests (~3800 lines), passing with 0 failures on two consecutive `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test err_syntax` runs (~21 s each).\n\n**`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/tmp/vwork/cov_err_syntax.tsv`** — 218 lines, exactly rows 259–476, no gaps/dupes: **203 covered, 15 uncoverable**.\n\n## Coverage approach\n\nFive drivers, each supplying a different filename so the `\"%s:%d: \"` prefix is compared: `js_ploadstring(fname)` (compile-only), `js_dostring` (`\"[string]\"`), `js_ploadstring`+`js_pcall`, `js_loadeval`+`js_call` via a `js_pcall`-protected cfunction (`iseval` axis), and `JSON.parse` (`\"JSON\"`). Each × `flags = 0` and `JS_STRICT`. Every observation records `(rc, typeof, error toString(), js_gettop, report-hook output)`. Executing drivers set `js_setlimit(runlimit)` because several shapes needed to reach an error site are legal infinite loops once they compile.\n\nNotable results pinned:\n- **js_Instruction (u16) overflow (row 411)** reachable two cheap ways I found instead of a 65535-instruction body: a statement on line ≥ **65536** (`emit` writes `F->lastline` raw) — boundary asserted exactly — and a call/`new` with ≥ **65536** arguments. Rows 427/428 via >65535 instructions before a jump is emitted/patched.\n- **JSON asymmetry preserved and pinned**: `JSON.parse('\"\\uZZZZ\"') === \"ZZZZ\"` (return value of `lexjsonescape` discarded, no error) vs `'\\uZZZZ'` → `SyntaxError: test.js:1: malformed escape sequence`.\n- **`try{}catch(class){}finally{}` accepted in sloppy mode, `try{}catch(class){}` rejected** — asserted for all 7 future words, in both libraries, plus the strict-mode inversion.\n- All 17 `INCREC` sites via 35 nesting constructs × ~60 depths, plus a threshold search 0..420 asserting C and Rust flip at the *identical* depth, plus a 1000-case mixed-nesting fuzz (all under `with_big_stack`).\n- Line-number subtleties: `\\r\\n` counts as one terminator; the ASI virtual `';'` carries the line *after* the terminator; `jsC_error` on an `AST_LIST` node reports line **0** (`LIST(h)` hard-codes 0) for rows 429/433; `'with'` in strict mode blames `stm->a`, not the keyword.\n- `%c` truncation: `/a/\\u{FF47}` → `illegal flag ...: G`; `/a/\\u{0100}` → message cut at the embedded NUL. Plus `snprintf(buf,256,...)` prefix truncation swept over filename lengths 230–270 × line-digit counts, and `%s` message truncation at 200–600 chars.\n- `js_strtod`: the ±511 clamp (and that `0e512` → **NaN**, since `1e511` itself is inf), the `INT_MAX/100` exponent guard, the 18-digit clamp, `mantSize==0` resetting `*endPtr`, bogus-exponent consumption, NULL `endPtr`.\n\n## Divergences found: none\n\nThe `src/*.rs` content hash was identical (`33ecfc68023115a1`) before and after — no Rust edits were needed. I read the Rust `jsY_error`/`jsP_error`/`jsC_error`, `lexnumber`, `lexescape`, `lexjson*`, `emitraw`/`emitjumpto`/`labelto`, `emitnumber`, `addlocal` and `js_strtod` against the C line by line; they are faithful, and every one of the 66 tests agreed first time except for four cases where **my own expectations** were wrong (all corrected to match the C, which was right): the line-0 `AST_LIST` blame for rows 429/433, `\"out of memory\"` being a raw `JS_TLITSTR` (no `Error: ` prefix), `0e512` → NaN, and the ASI virtual-`';'` line.\n\n## Uncoverable rows (15)\n\n| Rows | Reason |\n|---|---|\n| 288, 291, 309 | Unreachable: `jsY_expect(J, q/'/'/'\"')` runs only after a loop whose sole exit is `lexchar == q`. |\n| 384, 388 | Unreachable: `jsP_expect(J,'}')` after `caselist` / `script(J,'}')`, whose only exits are `lookahead == '}'`. |\n| 430, 431 | Unreachable: `cobject`'s key always comes from `propname` (3 types), `kv->type` always one of `EXP_PROP_VAL/GET/SET`. |\n| 436 | Unreachable: `cassignop2`'s switch is identical to `cassignop1`'s, which already accepted the same node. |\n| 439 | Unreachable: every node the parser can place in expression position has a `case` in `cexp`. |\n| 461, 462 | `assert()` → `abort()`; `c_src` builds without `-DNDEBUG`, so an aborting process can't be compared. Confirmed experimentally: `js_grisu2(-1.0, …)` aborts with `jsdtoa.c:387: minus: Assertion 'x.f >= y.f' failed`. Only out-of-contract inputs (0 / negative / non-finite) break it, and `jsV_numbertostring` special-cases all three. |\n| 463, 464, 465, 466 | C undefined behaviour, deliberately not tested per instructions (`powers_ten[343+k]` unbounded, `1 << -Mp.e` with `Mp.e >= 0`, unbounded `buffer[(*len)++]`, `se[9]`). |\n\nEach has a `///` doc comment naming `file:line`, and 288/291/309, 384/388, 430/431, 436/439 additionally have *pinning* tests (`t_unreachable_*`) that assert no input reaches them, so a future change that makes them reachable fails loudly. The `#if 0` dead block at `jslex.c:263-337` is noted as unreachable and pinned by `t_dead_lexnumber_block_is_unreachable` (its `\"malformed number\"` diagnostic can never be produced).\n\n## One thing to route elsewhere\n\n`tests/err_core.rs::t_dumpstrings_sentinel` (not my file) is **flaky under load**: it redirects process fd 1 while libtest writes `test <name> ... ok` progress lines from other threads in the same binary, and its line filter lets some of those through. It passes in isolation (6/6 runs) but fails reproducibly in a full `./run_tests.sh`. I verified it is not caused by my file — with `tests/err_syntax.rs` removed the full suite run had `err_core` green, so my binary only perturbs scheduling. The fix belongs in that test's filter (drop any line starting with `test `), which I left to its owner.\n\nSeparately, `tests/ll_regexp.rs::t_regexp_recursion_limit` runs for several minutes in a full-suite run — pre-existing, also not mine.","usage":{"total_tokens":354113,"tool_uses":115,"duration_ms":4422062},"uuid":"a0208e60-878b-4296-a2f1-bbea213a7497","session_id":"a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2"}
