` always one of `EXP_PROP_VAL/GET/SET`. |\n| 436 | Unreachable: `cassignop2`'s switch is identical to `cassignop1`'s, which already accepted the same node. |\n| 439 | Unreachable: every node the parser can place in expression position has a `case` in `cexp`. |\n| 461, 462 | `assert()` → `abort()`; `c_src` builds without `-DNDEBUG`, so an aborting process can't be compared. Confirmed experimentally: `js_grisu2(-1.0, …)` aborts with `jsdtoa.c:387: minus: Assertion 'x.f >= y.f' failed`. Only out-of-contract inputs (0 / negative / non-finite) break it, and `jsV_numbertostring` special-cases all three. |\n| 463, 464, 465, 466 | C undefined behaviour, deliberately not tested per instructions (`powers_ten[343+k]` unbounded, `1 << -Mp.e` with `Mp.e >= 0`, unbounded `buffer[(*len)++]`, `se[9]`). |\n\nEach has a `///` doc comment naming `file:line`, and 288/291/309, 384/388, 430/431, 436/439 additionally have *pinning* tests (`t_unreachable_*`) that assert no input reaches them, so a future change that makes them reachable fails loudly. The `#if 0` dead block at `jslex.c:263-337` is noted as unreachable and pinned by `t_dead_lexnumber_block_is_unreachable` (its `\"malformed number\"` diagnostic can never be produced).\n\n## One thing to route elsewhere\n\n`tests/err_core.rs::t_dumpstrings_sentinel` (not my file) is **flaky under load**: it redirects process fd 1 while libtest writes `test <name> ... ok` progress lines from other threads in the same binary, and its line filter lets some of those through. It passes in isolation (6/6 runs) but fails reproducibly in a full `./run_tests.sh`. I verified it is not caused by my file — with `tests/err_syntax.rs` removed the full suite run had `err_core` green, so my binary only perturbs scheduling. The fix belongs in that test's filter (drop any line starting with `test `), which I left to its owner.\n\nSeparately, `tests/ll_regexp.rs::t_regexp_recursion_limit` runs for several minutes in a full-suite run — pre-existing, also not mine."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa366551246145c23 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa366551246145c23', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 355191\ntool_uses: 115\nduration_ms: 4422064</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2","uuid":"b62a862d-eadc-4a9f-9316-c15f9426b84d","timestamp":"2026-08-23T12:47:55.242Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Work in /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust\n\nCONTEXT\nC->Rust translation of MuJS, verified by DIFFERENTIAL TESTING against the C.\n`c_src/` is the ground truth: READ-ONLY, never modify it and never create any\nfile under it. `src/*.rs` is the Rust translation built as a cdylib.\n\nCRITICAL BUILD RULE\n`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib (the tests reach it only via dlopen).\nALWAYS run `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test err_syntax`. Bare `cargo test`\nafter editing src/ trips a \"STALE Rust cdylib\" assertion. Never bypass it.\n\nREAD FIRST\n  ERRORS.md             - the error-surface table (933 rows). YOUR ROWS ARE 259-476.\n  tests/common/mod.rs   - the harness\n  tests/ll_lex.rs       - existing lexer-level differential tests\n  tests/gaps.rs         - protected-call probes and UB documentation style\n\nHARNESS API\n  libs() -> &'static Pair { c: Lib, rs: Lib }\n  new_state(l, flags) -> JS ; set_cur(l)\n  eval(l, flags, src) -> EvalResult { load_rc, call_rc, result, out, top }\n  dostring(l, flags, src) -> (c_int, String)\n  diff_eval(flags, src) / diff_dostring(flags, src)   // assert C == Rust\n  Lib::js_ploadstring / js_loadstring / js_loadeval / js_pcall / ...\n  cstr / from_c / Rng::new(seed) / with_big_stack(fn_item)\n\nYOUR TASK: PHASE C for ERRORS.md rows 259-476\n(jslex.c rows 259-322, jsparse.c 323-407, jscompile.c 408-460, jsdtoa.c 461-476)\n\nCreate ONE new file `tests/err_syntax.rs`. For EVERY row in that range, write a\ndifferential test that constructs that exact invalid input, calls BOTH\nlibraries, and asserts they produce the SAME error - the identical message text\n(these are all `SyntaxError: <file>:<line>: <msg>` style, so the FILENAME and\nLINE NUMBER are part of the observable result and must match too), the same\nreturn code, and the same stack state.\n\nRead the C first. Things you must get right:\n  - jsY_error / jsP_error / jsC_error all prefix `\"%s:%d: \"` with J->filename and\n    the CURRENT line. Use js_ploadstring with a known filename and put the error\n    on various lines (including after \\n, \\r, \\r\\n, \u2028, \u2029 line\n    terminators) so the line counter itself is compared.\n  - The bare `js_syntaxerror` calls in jscompile.c (js_Instruction overflow, jump\n    address overflow) have NO filename prefix. Reaching them needs a function\n    body large enough to overflow `unsigned short` (65535) instructions -- build\n    one programmatically. Also the two `js_evalerror` EvalError paths with the\n    hand-built `\"%s:%d: invalid use of 'eval'\"` prefix.\n  - JS_ASTLIMIT = 400 \"too much recursion\": there are 17 INCREC call sites in\n    jsparse.c. Drive nesting depth from 395 to 405 for EACH distinct nesting\n    construct (parens, arrays, objects, calls, member access, unary ops, binary\n    ops, ternary, blocks, if, while, for, function bodies, ...) so each site is\n    reached, and assert the exact depth at which each library starts failing.\n    THIS NEEDS with_big_stack.\n  - jsP_expect has ~44 call sites, each naming a different expected token in the\n    message (\"unexpected token: X (expected Y)\"). Construct an input for each.\n  - jslex.c: every number/string/regexp/comment error, every jsY_unescape and\n    lexescape failure, the JSON keyword per-character checks.\n    IMPORTANT ASYMMETRY to preserve: jslex.c:823-824 `lexjsonstring` calls\n    `lexjsonescape(J)` and DISCARDS its return value, so a malformed JSON\n    \\uXXXX raises NO error, unlike the JS string path which reports\n    \"malformed escape sequence\". Test both and keep the asymmetry.\n  - jscompile.c:990-995 vs :958-963: `try{}catch(class){}finally{}` is accepted\n    in sloppy mode while `try{}catch(class){}` is rejected. Assert that.\n  - jsdtoa.c rows: maxExponent 511 clamping (ERANGE -> inf / 0), the 18-digit\n    mantissa clamp, mantSize==0, the INT_MAX/100 exponent guard. Drive these\n    through `js_strtod` directly AND through JS number literals.\n  - Do NOT test jsdtoa's unchecked paths (js_fmtexp writing se[9] for 10-digit\n    exponents, cached_power index 343+k) -- those are C UB. Add `///` comments\n    naming file:line. tests/ll_num.rs already documents js_fmtexp.\n  - The `#if 0` dead block at jslex.c:263-337 is NOT reachable; note it.\n\nRun each source in BOTH strict and non-strict mode (`js_newstate` flags 0 and\nJS_STRICT) and through BOTH js_ploadstring and js_dostring and js_loadeval,\nbecause the `iseval` axis picks a different strictness and scope.\nUse MANY randomized inputs with a fixed seed where a row has a value dimension\n(e.g. random identifiers/numbers/escapes), plus the hand-built cases.\n\nRULES\n  - Every call must go through the harness (through the .so exports). Never call\n    Rust functions directly.\n  - NEVER call a throwing entry point outside a protected context\n    (js_pcall/js_ploadstring/js_dostring/js_pconstruct). A throw with trytop==0\n    aborts the process.\n  - Anything with deep nesting MUST be wrapped in with_big_stack.\n  - Keep each test under ~60s.\n\nDELIVERABLE 2: write a coverage map to\n  /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/tmp/vwork/cov_err_syntax.tsv\none line per ERRORS.md row you covered:\n  <row number>\\t<test function name>\nand for rows you could not cover:\n  <row number>\\tUNCOVERABLE: <reason>\n\nIF YOU FIND A DIVERGENCE\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix `src/*.rs` with the Edit tool (surgical edits only --\nnever Write over an existing src file). Re-read the C and make the Rust match.\n\nFINISH ONLY WHEN `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test err_syntax` passes with 0\nfailures twice in a row. Report: number of tests, how many of rows 259-476 are\ncovered, the uncoverable ones with reasons, and every divergence found+fixed.","agentId":"aa366551246145c23","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n**`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust/tests/err_syntax.rs`** — 66 differential tests (~3800 lines), passing with 0 failures on two consecutive `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test err_syntax` runs (~21 s each).\n\n**`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/tmp/vwork/cov_err_syntax.tsv`** — 218 lines, exactly rows 259–476, no gaps/dupes: **203 covered, 15 uncoverable**.\n\n## Coverage approach\n\nFive drivers, each supplying a different filename so the `\"%s:%d: \"` prefix is compared: `js_ploadstring(fname)` (compile-only), `js_dostring` (`\"[string]\"`), `js_ploadstring`+`js_pcall`, `js_loadeval`+`js_call` via a `js_pcall`-protected cfunction (`iseval` axis), and `JSON.parse` (`\"JSON\"`). Each × `flags = 0` and `JS_STRICT`. Every observation records `(rc, typeof, error toString(), js_gettop, report-hook output)`. Executing drivers set `js_setlimit(runlimit)` because several shapes needed to reach an error site are legal infinite loops once they compile.\n\nNotable results pinned:\n- **js_Instruction (u16) overflow (row 411)** reachable two cheap ways I found instead of a 65535-instruction body: a statement on line ≥ **65536** (`emit` writes `F->lastline` raw) — boundary asserted exactly — and a call/`new` with ≥ **65536** arguments. Rows 427/428 via >65535 instructions before a jump is emitted/patched.\n- **JSON asymmetry preserved and pinned**: `JSON.parse('\"\\uZZZZ\"') === \"ZZZZ\"` (return value of `lexjsonescape` discarded, no error) vs `'\\uZZZZ'` → `SyntaxError: test.js:1: malformed escape sequence`.\n- **`try{}catch(class){}finally{}` accepted in sloppy mode, `try