plain `strncmp` compares bytes.\n\n## Harness hardening added\n`diff_script` wraps `diff_dostring` with a non-vacuity guard: the script must reach completion (`rc == 0`), must not hit the report hook, must emit ≥ 5 lines, and must contain no `(eval):` SyntaxError or `ReferenceError`. That guard immediately caught 5 of my own generated fragments that were silently failing to parse identically in both libraries (bare `function(){}` / `{length:1,0:'a'}` passed to `eval`, and a reference to the non-existent `escape`), so those cases now actually exercise the rows they were written for."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae61ca1db3605c181 (use SendMessage with to: 'ae61ca1db3605c181', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 383602\ntool_uses: 152\nduration_ms: 4631765</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2","uuid":"bb23631b-c6a2-4451-a2a0-b593176b59ef","timestamp":"2026-08-23T14:11:58.779Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Work in /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/translated_rust\n\nCONTEXT\nC->Rust translation of MuJS, verified by DIFFERENTIAL TESTING against the C.\n`c_src/` is the ground truth: READ-ONLY, never modify it and never create any\nfile under it. `src/*.rs` is the Rust translation built as a cdylib.\n\nCRITICAL BUILD RULE\n`cargo test` does NOT rebuild the cdylib (the tests reach it only via dlopen).\nALWAYS run `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test err_misc`. Bare `cargo test` after\nediting src/ trips a \"STALE Rust cdylib\" assertion. Never bypass it.\n\nREAD FIRST\n  ERRORS.md            - the error-surface table (933 rows). YOUR ROWS ARE 731-933.\n  tests/common/mod.rs  - the harness\n  tests/err_core.rs    - a large existing Phase-C example in exactly the wanted\n                         style (protected-call probes, UB documentation, and a\n                         STDOUT_LOCK for anything writing to stdout)\n  tests/ll_regexp.rs   - existing low-level regexp differential tests\n  tests/ll_utf.rs      - existing utf differential tests\n\nHARNESS API\n  libs() -> &'static Pair { c: Lib, rs: Lib }\n  new_state(l, flags) -> JS ; set_cur(l)\n  eval / dostring / diff_eval / diff_dostring\n  Lib::<every exported symbol> incl. js_regcomp/js_regcompx/js_regexec/js_regfree\n  /js_regfreex/jsU_*/js_utflen/js_runeat; l.pred(name,j,idx); l.rune_pred(name,c);\n  l.rune_full(name,c); l.raw2::<T>(name)\n  Resub / REG_MAXSUB / REG_ICASE / REG_NEWLINE / REG_NOTBOL constants\n  cstr / from_c / out_clear / out_take / Rng::new(seed) / with_big_stack(fn_item)\n\nYOUR TASK: PHASE C for ERRORS.md rows 731-933\n(regexp.c 731-793, jsregexp.c 794-808, json.c 809-824, jsdate.c 825-879,\n jsfunction.c 880-889, jsmath.c 890-902, jsrepr.c 903-910, utf.c 911-933)\n\nCreate ONE new file `tests/err_misc.rs`. For EVERY row in that range write a\ndifferential test that constructs that exact invalid input, calls BOTH\nlibraries, and asserts they return the SAME error / sentinel - the identical\nerror string or return value, not merely \"both failed\".\n\nRead the C first. Things to get exactly right:\n  - regexp.c has its OWN error mechanism: `die()` sets `g->error` and longjmps to\n    the `setjmp` in `regcompx` (regexp.c:903), which frees everything, sets\n    `*errorp` and returns NULL. There are 32 die() sites producing 18 distinct\n    message strings. Construct a pattern that reaches EACH SITE (not just each\n    message - e.g. \"unmatched '('\" has 4 distinct sites and\n    \"unterminated escape sequence\" has 4). Assert the exact `*errorp` string and\n    that the return is NULL, with errorp both non-NULL and NULL.\n  - The regexp limits: REG_MAXPROG 32768 (\"program too large\", 3 sites),\n    REG_MAXREC 4096 (regexec returns -1), REG_MAXSPAN 64\n    (\"too many character class ranges\"), REG_MAXCLASS 128\n    (\"too many character classes\"), REPINF 255 (\"numeric overflow\", 2 sites),\n    REG_MAXSUB 16 (\"too many captures\"). Drive each boundary from below to above\n    and assert both libraries flip at the identical size.\n    NOTE tests/ll_regexp.rs already has t_regexp_maxprog_boundary; do the others.\n  - `match()` returns 1 for \"no match\" from 22 distinct sites including a\n    `default:` unknown-opcode path. Cover the reachable ones.\n  - jsregexp.c: `Error \"regexec failed\"` at jsregexp.c:77 and :126 when\n    regexec returns -1 (drive via a deep-recursion subject), plus lastIndex\n    handling. NOTE js_Regexp::last is `unsigned short`, so lastIndex = 70000\n    reads back 4464 and -1 reads back 65535 - assert that.\n  - json.c: every parse rejection and every stringify rejection (cyclic\n    structures, bad reviver, non-callable replacer, ...).\n  - jsdate.c: every invalid-date / range rejection, toISOString on an invalid\n    date, Date.parse failures, and the getters/formatters on a NaN time.\n    IMPORTANT: local-time functions depend on TZ. Both libraries run in the same\n    process so they see the same TZ; pin TZ ONCE process-wide in a OnceLock\n    before either library caches LocalTZA (tests/js_builtins.rs shows how) and\n    assert both agree. Do NOT set TZ per-library.\n  - jsfunction.c: Function constructor / apply / bind / call argument validation.\n  - jsmath.c, jsrepr.c: every failure return.\n    NOTE jsrepr.c:231 writes \"[iterator \" with NO closing bracket, and\n    jsrepr.c:129 emits the \", \" separator BEFORE checking js_hasindex so sparse\n    arrays repr as \"[1, , 3]\". Reproduce the C; do not fix it.\n  - utf.c: every failure return - chartorune's Runeerror (0xFFFD) paths,\n    runetochar out-of-range, runelen invalid, the tolowerrune_full /\n    toupperrune_full NULL returns, isalpharune/islowerrune/isupperrune misses.\n    tests/ll_utf.rs already sweeps these broadly; here focus on asserting the\n    exact SENTINEL VALUES the rows name (Runeerror, NULL, 0, the byte counts).\n\nALSO cover generic FFI boundary abuse: NULL pointers (js_regcomp pattern=NULL is\nUB - check the C first; js_regexec prog=NULL, string=NULL, sub=NULL), zero and\noversized lengths, values one step past each range, and OUT-OF-RANGE ENUM VALUES\nacross the FFI (cflags/eflags with no valid variant: negative, 8, 255, INT_MAX,\nINT_MIN; JS_REGEXP_* combinations beyond 7).\n\nRULES\n  - Every call must go through the harness (through the .so exports). Never call\n    Rust functions directly.\n  - MANY randomized inputs where a row has a value dimension, fixed seed.\n  - NEVER call a throwing entry point outside a protected context\n    (js_pcall/js_pconstruct/js_ploadstring/js_dostring). js_newregexp THROWS on a\n    bad pattern - use the cfunction-probe pattern (tests/gaps.rs\n    `newregexp_probe`). A throw with trytop==0 aborts the process.\n  - Deep regexp recursion MUST use with_big_stack, and prefer LINEAR patterns\n    (`a*b` over a long subject) to reach REG_MAXREC: catastrophic-backtracking\n    patterns like `(a*)*b` also hit it but take hours. See\n    tests/ll_regexp.rs::t_regexp_recursion_limit.\n  - Some C paths are UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR. Do NOT test those; add a `///` comment\n    naming file:line and why. Verify against the C before relying on this.\n  - If ANY test writes to stdout, hold a file-local STDOUT_LOCK for its whole\n    duration - see tests/err_core.rs. Otherwise the suite is flaky.\n  - Keep each test under ~60s.\n\nDELIVERABLE 2: write a coverage map to\n  /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-0eE2Fw/tmp/vwork/cov_err_misc.tsv\none line per ERRORS.md row 731-933:\n  <row number>\\t<test function name>\nor for rows you could not cover:\n  <row number>\\tUNCOVERABLE: <reason>\n\nIF YOU FIND A DIVERGENCE\nThe C is ALWAYS right. Fix `src/*.rs` with the Edit tool (surgical edits only --\nnever Write over an existing src file). Re-read the C and make the Rust match.\n\nFINISH ONLY WHEN `timeout 600 ./run_tests.sh --test err_misc` passes with 0\nfailures twice in a row. Report: number of tests, how 