{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01TVXizN79QGqEY8SXxfJ7V8","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n# SYMBOLS.md — exported-symbol parity (C `libmujs.so` vs Rust `libmujs.so`)\n\nGenerated mechanically with `nm -D --defined-only` on both shared objects\n(`c_src/build/libmujs.so` and `target/debug/libmujs.so`).\n\n## Totals\n\n| | count |\n|---|---|\n| symbols exported by C `.so` | 237 |\n| symbols exported by Rust `.so` | 237 |\n| **C symbols MISSING from Rust `.so`** | **0** |\n| extra symbols only in Rust `.so` | 0 |\n| undefined **non-libc** symbols in Rust `.so` | **0** |\n\nEvery undefined entry in the Rust `.so` is a libc / libgcc import\n(`malloc`, `realloc`, `memcpy`, `memset`, `_setjmp`, `longjmp`, `pow`, `sin`,\n`gmtime`, `mktime`, `snprintf`, `_Unwind_*`, ...). The C `.so` imports the same\nset plus `ceil`/`floor`/`fmod`/`sqrt`/`__assert_fail`; the Rust build lowers\nthose four to instructions instead of libm calls.\n\n### Note: variadic and macro-generated symbols\n\nThe 8 printf-style variadic entry points (`js_error`, `js_evalerror`,\n`js_rangeerror`, `js_referenceerror`, `js_syntaxerror`, `js_typeerror`,\n`js_urierror`, `jsC_error`) cannot be written as C-variadic functions in stable\nRust. They are exported from `src/lib.rs` as `#[naked]` x86-64 SysV\ntrampolines (`va_trampoline!`) that materialise a `va_list` and tail into the\n`*_va` worker in `src/jserror.rs` / `src/jscompile.rs`. The\n`js_new*error` family is generated by the `DERROR!` macro in `src/jserror.rs`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a62d17ad-8528-487e-8b6a-23ad0ded9ac2","uuid":"1c09b9f6-a804-4515-9273-9c17f7ec0ad9","timestamp":"2026-08-23T14:37:31.486Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"ok\n# SYMBOLS.md — exported-symbol parity (C `libmujs.so` vs Rust `libmujs.so`)\n\nGenerated mechanically with `nm -D --defined-only` on both shared objects\n(`c_src/build/libmujs.so` and `target/debug/libmujs.so`).\n\n## Totals\n\n| | count |\n|---|---|\n| symbols exported by C `.so` | 237 |\n| symbols exported by Rust `.so` | 237 |\n| **C symbols MISSING from Rust `.so`** | **0** |\n| extra symbols only in Rust `.so` | 0 |\n| undefined **non-libc** symbols in Rust `.so` | **0** |\n\nEvery undefined entry in the Rust `.so` is a libc / libgcc import\n(`malloc`, `realloc`, `memcpy`, `memset`, `_setjmp`, `longjmp`, `pow`, `sin`,\n`gmtime`, `mktime`, `snprintf`, `_Unwind_*`, ...). The C `.so` imports the same\nset plus `ceil`/`floor`/`fmod`/`sqrt`/`__assert_fail`; the Rust build lowers\nthose four to instructions instead of libm calls.\n\n### Note: variadic and macro-generated symbols\n\nThe 8 printf-style variadic entry points (`js_error`, `js_evalerror`,\n`js_rangeerror`, `js_referenceerror`, `js_syntaxerror`, `js_typeerror`,\n`js_urierror`, `jsC_error`) cannot be written as C-variadic functions in stable\nRust. They are exported from `src/lib.rs` as `#[naked]` x86-64 SysV\ntrampolines (`va_trampoline!`) that materialise a `va_list` and tail into the\n`*_va` worker in `src/jserror.rs` / `src/jscompile.rs`. The\n`js_new*error` family is generated by the `DERROR!` macro in `src/jserror.rs`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
